      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ottsville was named in 1814 after Michael Ott, the first postmaster, replacing its earlier name Red Hill because of the red shale ridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ottsville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first log church in Ottsville dates to 1738, later replaced by a stone building in 1766, reflecting early Presbyterian roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ottsville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A historic cemetery beside Luke's United Church of Christ holds graves of Revolutionary and Civil War veterans, with the oldest marker from 1749.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ottsville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Route 611’s bypass now diverts traffic from the village center, preserving Ottsville’s rural character.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ottsville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area’s fertile silty loam soils, part of the Neshaminy-Penlaw series, have long supported the region’s agricultural heritage.",
